@@ -22752,3 +22752,25 @@ Direct request: reduce how often quests drop eggs, "very rare" as the target. Re
 **Confirmed unaffected, explicitly out of scope:** raid egg drops (authored per-encounter in raid loot tables, not this flat-percentage system, so untouched) and chain-completion guaranteed eggs (`ChainDef.rewardEgg`, a fixed one-time grant on specific story beats, not a per-quest roll). Only the ordinary quest-board roll changed.
 
 **Verified:** `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx vite build --config vite.web.config.ts` both pass clean against a fresh clone with this patch applied. No `SAVE_VERSION` bump needed -- tuning-data-only change, no `GameState` shape touched.
+
+
+### Alchemist/Enchanter first-visit shimmer (patch 0291)
+
+```discord-update
+Dev Update | Patch 0291
+- Added: the Alchemist and Enchanter sub-tabs now glow with the same gold shimmer border used elsewhere, until you visit each one for the first time
+```
+
+**New-tester feedback, direct quote:** "important things weren't immedently obvious (ie, Vendors such as Alchemist and Enchanter)." On a first playthrough, Alchemist and Enchanter are just two more plain sub-tab buttons sitting next to the always-open Blacksmith tab -- nothing marks them as worth a look, so a new player can go a long time never realizing they exist. Requested fix: reuse the existing gold shimmer border (the same one already used elsewhere) to highlight both, clearing permanently the first time each is opened.
+
+**Reused the exact shimmer, not a lookalike -- same pattern as patch 0288's quest-card spotlight.** `.nav-tab-unread::after`'s rotating conic-gradient ring (the registered `--nav-tab-shimmer-angle` custom property plus the `nav-tab-shimmer-rotate` keyframe) now has a third sibling rule, `.btn-subtab.subtab-spotlight::after`, sharing the same custom property and keyframe so the rotation and gradient stops stay byte-identical rather than drifting into a second, subtly different effect. Only the ring's thickness and `border-radius` differ, sized down for a `.btn-subtab` button (`border-radius: var(--radius)`) instead of a nav button or a full quest card.
+
+**A deliberate exception to the sub-tab-dot precedent, not an oversight.** `.btn-subtab.subtab-unread` (patch 0191) is a small pulsing dot rather than a ring specifically because several sub-tabs can be simultaneously flagged "unread" via the general notification system at once (Vendors has three sub-tabs, Lore and Statistics three each), and a full ring on every one of them together would compete with itself for attention. That reasoning doesn't apply here: at most two buttons (Alchemist, Enchanter) can ever carry the new class, Blacksmith is deliberately excluded (it's the default-open tab a new player already lands on, nothing to discover there), and each clears permanently the instant that vendor's page is opened once -- a one-time "look over here" nudge, not a recurring signal that can re-arm. Documented this reasoning directly in both the CSS comment and the new `GameState` fields' own comment so a future patch author doesn't "fix" the inconsistency by merging it into the dot.
+
+**New `GameState.hasSeenAlchemist` / `hasSeenEnchanter: boolean`** (`SAVE_VERSION` 55 -> 56, migration 55 added). Each defaults `false` for a brand-new save (`SaveManager.createInitialState`) so the shimmer shows until that vendor's tab is opened once. The migration grandfathers every *existing* save to `true` for both fields rather than `false` -- this is a first-timer's nudge, not a retroactive nag aimed at a tester who's already had the Alchemist open a hundred times; re-arming the shimmer for someone already deep into a save would just read as a bug.
+
+**New `GameEngine.acknowledgeVendorFirstVisit(vendorId)`**, called from `VendorsPanel`'s existing per-tab `useEffect` (the same one that already calls `acknowledgeTab('vendors', tab)` on mount and on every switch) -- so it fires whether the player got to that vendor by clicking the button directly or via a notification's own deep link (`requestTab`'s `subTab`), either way counting as "seen." No-op for `blacksmith` and for a vendor already marked seen, so it's safe to call unconditionally on every tab change rather than gating the call site itself.
+
+**`VendorsPanel.tsx`:** each vendor button's `isSpotlighted` check is `(v.id === 'alchemist' && !hasSeenAlchemist) || (v.id === 'enchanter' && !hasSeenEnchanter)`, adding `subtab-spotlight` to the button's existing className alongside the pre-existing `on`/`subtab-unread` classes -- all three can combine on the same button without conflict, since they're independent visual layers (background fill, dot, ring).
+
+**Verified:** `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx vite build --config vite.web.config.ts` both pass clean against a fresh clone with this patch applied. `SAVE_VERSION` bumped 55 -> 56 for the two new fields -- migration 55 added, defaulting existing saves to `true` for both (see reasoning above). Worth a real playtest: on a brand-new guild, confirm Alchemist and Enchanter both shimmer in the Vendors tab strip before either has been opened, and confirm each ring clears independently and permanently the instant that specific vendor's page is opened (not both at once, and not on a reload once cleared).
